          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Would changing when I eat help?",
          "answer": "The search found a diet note on reflux (*Eating with reflux: meal size, timing, and finding individual triggers*, General clinical reference and gastroenterology educational summary). It says that for people whose symptoms mainly happen at night, one change \"tends to do more than any food substitution.\" The passage starts partway through, so I can't tell from it what that change is, and it doesn't clearly say that eating earlier helps.\n\nThe same note says weight reduction has the strongest supporting evidence of any lifestyle measure, if your weight is above the healthy range. It also says that cutting out many common trigger foods (such as coffee, alcohol, citrus, tomato, mint, fatty or spicy food, and fizzy drinks) has weak evidence. It describes a more useful approach: work out your own triggers, rather than avoiding everything.\n\nThe search didn't return the full advice on meal timing, so a clinician or dietitian is the best person to ask whether changing when you eat would help you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
