          "question": "And is it ever a sign of something worse?",
          "answer": "Heartburn can sometimes be linked to other conditions, though the search results don't show that it is usually a sign of something worse. Reflux is common, and the pattern, especially its link to meals and lying down, is more informative than how bad it feels (*Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)*, General clinical reference).\n\nSome symptoms that can come with reflux are less specific. These include a persistent cough, hoarseness, throat clearing, a feeling of a lump in the throat, dental erosion, and non-cardiac chest pain. Many people with these turn out to have another cause, so a clinician should look at them rather than assume reflux (same source).\n\nHeart-related pain can feel like heartburn, and taking antacids does not rule out a heart cause (*Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack*, Emergency medicine and cardiology society guidance). Seek emergency care straight away for chest pain or pressure, especially with breathlessness, sweating, nausea, or pain in the jaw, back, or upper belly.\n\nBleeding from the upper digestive tract is another reason for urgent care. Call emergency services if you vomit blood or something like coffee grounds, or pass black, tarry stools (*Gastrointestinal bleeding*, Gastroenterology and emergency medicine society guidance).\n\nThe guideline on reflux management says some \"alarm features\" change the approach, so a clinician should check for them. My search did not return that list, so I can't tell you what they are. The automated check found no emergency pattern in your description, but it says that does not mean nothing is wrong. Please book a clinician visit if the heartburn keeps happening or gets worse, or if you notice any new or unexplained symptoms.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
